                How to build c-ci using MSVC or Visual Studio
               =================================================



  How to build using MSVC from the command line
  ---------------------------------------------

  Open a command prompt window and ensure that the environment is properly
  set up in order to use MSVC or Visual Studio compiler tools.

  Change to c-ci source folder where Makefile.msvc file is located and run:

  > nmake -f Makefile.msvc

  This will build all c-ci libraries as well as three sample programs.

  Once the above command has finished a new folder named MSVCXX will exist
  below the folder where makefile.msvc is found. The name of the folder
  depends on the MSVC compiler version being used to build c-ci.

  Below the MSVCXX folder there will exist four folders named 'cci',
  'ahost', and 'adig'. The 'cci' folder is the one that
  holds the c-ci libraries you have just generated, the other three
  hold sample programs that use the libraries.

  The above command builds four versions of the c-ci library, dynamic
  and static versions and each one in release and debug flavours. Each
  of these is found in folders named dll-release, dll-debug, lib-release,
  and lib-debug, which hang from the 'cci' folder mentioned above. Each
  sample program also has folders with the same names to reflect which
  library version it is using.


  How to install using MSVC from the command line
  -----------------------------------------------

  In order to allow easy usage of c-ci libraries it may be convenient to
  install c-ci libraries and header files to a common subdirectory tree.

  Once that c-ci libraries have been built using procedure described above,
  use same command prompt window to define environment variable INSTALL_DIR
  to designate the top subdirectory where installation of c-ci libraries and
  header files will be done.

  > set INSTALL_DIR=c:\c-ci

  Afterwards, run following command to actually perform the installation:

  > nmake -f Makefile.msvc install

  Installation procedure will copy c-ci libraries to subdirectory 'lib' and
  c-ci header files to subdirectory 'include' below the INSTALL_DIR subdir.

  When environment variable INSTALL_DIR is not defined, installation is done
  to c-ci source folder where Makefile.msvc file is located.



  Relationship between c-ci library file names and versions
  -----------------------------------------------------------

  c-ci static release library version files:

      libcci.lib -> static release library

  c-ci static debug library version files:

      libccid.lib -> static debug library

  c-ci dynamic release library version files:

      cci.dll -> dynamic release library
      cci.lib -> import library for the dynamic release library
      cci.exp -> export file for the dynamic release library

  c-ci dynamic debug library version files:

      ccid.dll -> dynamic debug library
      ccid.lib -> import library for the dynamic debug library
      ccid.exp -> export file for the dynamic debug library
      ccid.pdb -> debug symbol file for the dynamic debug library


  How to use c-ci static libraries
  ----------------------------------

  When using the c-ci static library in your program, you will have to
  define preprocessor symbol CI_STATICLIB while building your program,
  otherwise you will get errors at linkage stage.


Have Fun!

